00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.680
Maura, we have a job for you.

77
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:03.040
Done.

78
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.780
Not so much a question, but rather a feminist rant.

79
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:22.660
I hate that Robert introduces his wife to the bullpen as Mrs. Robert California so, so much.

80
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.960
She corrects them immediately by asking to be called Susan, but this demonstrates an epidemic

81
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.660
that I am so repulsed by.

82
00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.960
Why is it always Mr. and Mrs., the man's last name?

83
00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.880
I feel like there's a deep dive here just waiting to be unpacked and ranted about.

84
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.160
Hashtag downwiththepatriarchy.

85
00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.460
Kate, I like your fire.

86
00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:42.920
I'm liking it.

87
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.900
I deep-dived it for you, and here you go.

88
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:46.280
Okay.

89
00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.040
Women did not always take their husband's last name.

90
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.680
In fact, last names didn't even matter at first, because back in the Dark Ages, I guess

91
00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.760
populations were so small that you just went by your first name.

92
00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:03.600
But later, when last names mattered, that's when women started taking the last name of their

93
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:04.020
husbands.

94
00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.560
And according to several articles, including one from Brides.com, the reason is because

95
00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.720
women did not hold any legal rights on their own.

96
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.120
They couldn't own land.

97
00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:15.000
That's right.

98
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:15.480
Things like that.

99
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:16.340
Yes.

100
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:22.820
Or if you were injured as a woman and someone was to be compensated for your injury, it was

101
00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:28.400
either your father, because you were his property, or your husband, once you married.

102
00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.000
Women could not enter into contracts.

103
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.800
However, they could be employed, but their husband collected their wages.

104
00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:40.000
Also, the husband held the right to sexual access to their wife.

105
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.140
So this was all, you know, back in the day.

106
00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:44.840
Kind of sounds like The Handmaid's Tale.

107
00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:46.700
Oh, a lot like The Handmaid's Tale.

108
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.600
One of the first things that happens when they're slowly taking over is that they close all the

109
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.780
women's banking accounts and have their funds transferred to their husband's accounts.

110
00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:56.220
Yeah.

111
00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:56.540
Yeah.

112
00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.580
Creepy stuff.

113
00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:58.000
Right.

114
00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.680
Here's something I found out.

115
00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:06.200
Did you know that prior to the 1970s, there were laws in the United States that said women

116
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:12.200
could not get a driver's license, a passport, or register to vote unless a woman took her

117
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.160
husband's last name?

118
00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.940
If you were unmarried, you could.

119
00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.520
But if you were married, you could only do those things if you took your husband's last

120
00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:20.740
name.

121
00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:21.240
Hmm.

122
00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.720
It was the Supreme Court case of Dunn v.

123
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.720
Palermo that struck down a Tennessee law that required

124
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.300
women to take their husband's last name in order to vote, and that is when it finally

125
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:33.660
ended.

126
00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:40.980
And that is also when the little, what do you call it, the prefix, Ms. came into play.

127
00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.640
Because you could be a Miss, a Mrs., or a Ms.

128
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:44.660
Mm-hmm.

129
00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:46.860
Want to hear about a badass lady?

130
00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:47.560
Yeah.

131
00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:48.860
Amelia Earhart?

132
00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:49.620
Mm-hmm.

133
00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.780
She refused to go by her husband's last name.

134
00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:57.880
And after her first solo transatlantic flight, the New York Times wrote a big article about

135
00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.740
her, and the whole time they referred to her as Mrs. Putnam.

136
00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:02.780
So she wrote them a letter.

137
00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.180
Yes, she did.

138
00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.900
She said, as previously stated.

139
00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:08.520
Ooh!

140
00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:14.520
I mean, we all know if someone says that, they're like, listen, this is my polite way of saying

141
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:15.400
go F yourself.

142
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:16.040
Yes.

143
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.140
I will say that phrase was not in her letter, but it was implied.

144
00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:26.160
She did say, like, as stated in my previous letters to you, I would like you to refer to

145
00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:31.740
me by my professional name, Amelia Earhart, and not Mrs. Putnam.

146
00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:32.620
Yeah.

147
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.340
So I took my husband's last name.

148
00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.840
I am Jenna Kirk.

149
00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:39.100
I took his last name.

150
00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.380
And approximately 70% of women still take their husband's last names when they get married.

151
00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.600
20% keep their maiden names.

152
00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:51.300
And 10% hyphenate their name, or they make up a new name, a new last name together.

153
00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.490
I'm in the 20%.

154
00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:53.660
Okay.

155
00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.680
I did not take my husband's last name.

156
00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:57.700
I am just Angela Kinsey.

157
00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:03.000
Although I absolutely love being Josh's wife, I am the last of four girls.

158
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:10.040
And I remember when my dad was alive telling him, dad, I'll keep the Kinsey for you.

159
00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.700
Oh, that's so sweet.

160
00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:15.120
So his name would live on at least a little longer through me, right?

161
00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:15.380
Yeah.

162
00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:15.780
Yeah.

163
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.300
And it meant a lot to him.

164
00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.240
So that was something I wanted to do for my dad.

165
00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:21.900
I love that lady.

166
00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:22.480
Yeah.

167
00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.000
And Josh doesn't mind at all.

168
00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.200
He knows I'm also Mrs. Snyder.

169
00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:32.600
Well, you know, very occasionally, men will take their wives' last names.

170
00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.300
But it is much more difficult to do it.

171
00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:41.780
Like when a woman gets married, on your marriage certificate, there's just a line where you can fill in.

172
00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.160
That line isn't there for the men, I guess?

173
00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:58.380
Because only in Georgia, Hawaii, Iowa, Massachusetts, New York, and North Dakota is it as easy for a man to take his wife's last name upon marriage as the other way around.

174
00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.120
So there's my deep dive for you, Kate.

175
00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:03.180
Well, I loved it.

176
00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:06.060
And right here in this room, the two of us represent two different sides.

177
00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:06.640
We do.

178
00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:07.020
Yeah.

179
00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:13.060
Ultimately, how I feel about it is, whatever is comfortable between you and your partner ain't nobody else's business.

180
00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:13.880
Yeah.

181
00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:14.500
Yeah.

182
00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:16.400
I like being Jenna Kirk.

183
00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:16.900
Yeah.

184
00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:18.060
All right.

185
00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:19.540
Are you ready, everybody?

186
